{
    "format": "qucs-s multiphysics 3d 1",
    "title": "A through-silicon via",
    "description": "A copper via 5 µm across and 25 µm deep through a silicon die, in its oxide liner: its resistance between its ends (ec.R) and the heat a hot pad on its top sends down it and, through the liner, into the silicon beside it (ht.Rth), the die's back held at 20 °C. Its resistance is ρh/(πa²), as G. Katti, M. Stucchi, K. De Meyer and W. Dehaene give it (IEEE Trans. Electron Devices 57, 256, 2010): 21.2 mΩ here, order 2 on its arcs within 0.1 %. Its copper alone would hold the heat at h/(kπa²), 3183 K/W; the silicon beside it carries part of it down, past the liner's ln(b/a)/(2πkh): 1332 K/W. Qucs-S's tests hold all three to their formulas.",
    "parameters": [
        {"name": "a", "expression": "2.5[um]", "description": "the via's radius"},
        {"name": "tl", "expression": "0.2[um]", "description": "its liner's thickness"},
        {"name": "h", "expression": "25[um]", "description": "its depth: the die's thickness"},
        {"name": "P", "expression": "1[mW]", "description": "the heat into its top"}
    ],
